WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.906
The Purdue Enterprise Reference Architecture is an industrial architecture model that organizes systems according to the functions they perform, from the physical process through control and operations to enterprise planning.

00:00:12.906 --> 00:00:20.486
In security discussions, the term Purdue Model usually refers to this layered view of an industrial environment.

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:26.018
The model gives engineers, operators, architects, and security teams a shared vocabulary.

00:00:26.018 --> 00:00:38.924
Instead of describing a network only by subnets or vendor products, teams can discuss where a function belongs, what it depends on, and how close it is to physical control.

00:00:38.924 --> 00:00:47.324
A controller at Level 1 has a different operational purpose and consequence than an enterprise application at Level 4.

00:00:47.324 --> 00:00:55.313
OT security practitioners use the model to reason about trust boundaries, communication paths, access, monitoring, and incident containment.

00:00:55.313 --> 00:00:57.977
It helps expose questionable architecture.

00:00:57.977 --> 00:01:06.786
For example, a user workstation should not normally communicate directly with a field controller simply because both devices use Ethernet.

00:01:06.786 --> 00:01:13.136
The Purdue Model is not a product, a protocol, or a complete security design.

00:01:13.136 --> 00:01:21.946
It does not prescribe every firewall rule, and assigning a system to a level does not make that system secure.

00:01:21.946 --> 00:01:29.526
The model must be combined with asset inventory, current diagrams, operational requirements, risk analysis, and engineering judgment.

00:01:29.526 --> 00:01:41.203
Modern environments also include cloud analytics, wireless devices, vendor platforms, remote operations, and industrial Internet of Things systems that may not fit neatly into a simple stack.

00:01:41.203 --> 00:01:50.422
Use Purdue as a reference for asking better questions, not as a perfect map that forces every architecture into identical boxes.
